David everybody talks about  neoliberalism can you talk about what  that actually means what does it mean to  be neoliberal and is it a smear okay the  the second part is easy too easier to  answer people who are against  neoliberalism tend to use it as a smear  people who are neoliberal tend not to  use the term at all it's sort of like  when when the right talks about social  Darwinism is it is a terrible thing  nobody on the Left uses the term it's a  term that mostly exists to smear people  you disagree with neoliberal is a thing  it's it's much more a real practical  definable thing in modern politics but  it's mostly people criticizing  neoliberalism that use the term  so let's first try to figure out what it  originally meant and then we can think  about how it's applied now neoliberal  sort of means small-l liberalism with a  tendency to support free-market  capitalism so I don't mean capital L  liberalism like the Liberal Party in the  UK or liberalism as progressivism but I  mean liberalism as the the sort of  nineteenth-century  laissez-faire liberalism a combined or  modernized with some particular  perspectives that include deregulation  of businesses the Reagan tax cuts were  sort of part of more modern 20th century  neoliberalism more defense spending  often is in line with modern  neoliberalism what else deregulation of  the financial industry deregulation of  trucking and banking and Airlines so  there's if we imagine Neal that we if we  imagine liberalism as sort of a late  19th century movement neoliberalism is  the way in which it was restructured and  updated starting sort of in the early  1970s Mecca nama CLE it's it's typically  just interwove on with supply-side  economics the sort of trickle-down tax  cuts for the rich for good forever  buddy type of stuff it's not just  Republicans that are neoliberal or do  neoliberal politics Bill Clinton passing  NAFTA the North American Free Trade  Agreement that was a widely accepted  neoliberal thing to do but but the sort  of underlying ideas are privatization of  things that should be or were public  deregulation cutting government spending  under the pretense of the debt as we've  talked about that political football and  turning over more and more of the  economy to private companies and that's  where it kind of gets into foreign  policy because right now in the United  States when critics of neoliberalism  talk about neoliberalism they're often  talking more about foreign policy than  they are tax cuts although certainly as  I mentioned supply-side economics and  tax cuts for the rich are part of the  early neoliberal ideas of the 70s and  80s the Iraq war and Afghanistan war  came from neoliberal ideology upbringing  order as we see it to other regions the  idea of spreading democracy like for  example as the 2003 Iraq war claim to  want to do in Iraq that is a neoliberal  idea what makes this a little difficult  is that there's actually a split within  neoliberalism about interventionism  generally speaking neoliberalism is for  foreign intervention but it doesn't  always mean military intervention so a  lot of the people on the right who are  against military interventions in many  cases under sort of libertarian ideas it  doesn't mean they aren't neoliberal they  often just favor intervention via soft  power democracy quiet support for coos  and sort of other geopolitical tactics  that are out there so there's not one  definition of neoliberalism anytime that  we have an ideology that is often  defined by its opponents we have to be  sort of cognizant of the fact that that  that is a thing  in thinking about what is part of it and  what isn't part of it but that's sort of  a basic structure for what neoliberalism  means in practice right now in the 21st  century
 

